"""Curator-authored retrospective questions/answers grounded in captured public messages.
No executed trajectory is invented; suggested disconfirmation is explicitly prospective.
"""
CASES=[
('497d71d94eed','interpret_benchmark','tradeoff',
 'A tiled FP8 projection is 1.6–3.8x faster per call and improves forward throughput, yet teacher KLD rises from 0.086035 to 0.086674. Explain what changed numerically and what admission claim is justified.',
 'The source says both routes reconstruct the same BF16 weight operands, but a tiled matrix-unit reduction need not have the GEMV/GEMM reference reduction order. Equal operands therefore do not prove equal final logits. The KLD increase is a reported quality cost, even if small; it must not be described as a quality improvement. The accepted arm targets 9–128 rows and preserves the reference route for teacher capture. Its per-call speed ratios and smaller forward gains are different scopes. A follow-up should compare full logits and task quality under a declared tolerance across that row boundary and hardware, then run paired end-to-end timing. Those checks are recommendations; this library has not reproduced the historical author measurements.'),
('8e991af832ac','select_dispatch','tradeoff',
 'The new unequal-width packed attention avoids rebuilding a dense cache but loses at 2048 keys. Why is removing that buffer insufficient to justify a universal dispatch?',
 'Eliminating cache reconstruction saves traffic, but the new tile also changes occupancy, staging and arithmetic work. The source reports that dense attention remains faster at 2048 keys and admits the packed matrix-unit route only from 4096. Query/key width 192 and value width 128 require separate tile dimensions, and a short-history crossover cannot be inferred from a long-history win. The author also reports a precision distinction from the SIMD route; validate against the declared FP32 truth rather than calling the new route bitwise identical. Preserve the explicit fallback/probe and measure both sides of the crossover on each intended device. The code credits mlx-serve/Splash, so this is a ported implementation family, not an independent algorithm discovery.'),
('d1408a57c23c','reject_optimization','tradeoff',
 'Review the rejected experiments and accepted changes in this EXL3 prefill bundle. How should an agent avoid attributing the entire result to whichever isolated kernel benchmark is largest?',
 'Separate each changed kernel or dispatch mechanism from the bundle-level serving result. The captured message rejects dropping low-weight experts because it adds 0.0029 KLD for only 2.8% of slots; W8A8 offers twice the MMA rate but costs more in weight conversion; folding the last prompt token into a chunk breaks cold-versus-warm byte equality. These are specific disconfirming results, not missing successes to suppress. An accepted verify-width route must retain its source eligibility and parity contract, while the complete patch also changes prefill and admission behavior. A controlled ablation would hold the rest of the binary, input shape, device and cache state fixed and compare each mechanism alone, then the bundle. No such ablation is invented here. The exact full edit exceeds the archival context limit in this collection and is preserved as source evidence rather than silently truncated into a supposedly complete optimization task.'),
('1ea9492abc01','explain_change','positive',
 'How can a rate-generic EXL3 unpacker recover specialized-reader performance while preserving weights, and what does the slower generic alternative teach?',
 'The author reports that a generic reader was about 40% slower in the measured setting. The adopted scheme uses fixed-width word/funnel reads, including the third word when a code straddles it, while covering the supported n32–64 rates such as n36 and n42. The key contract is exact bit extraction and codebook indexing; faster arithmetic is irrelevant if a boundary reads a different code. A useful follow-up would exhaust bit offsets and rates against an independent unpack oracle, include end-of-buffer guards, and compare complete layer timings at both small and large row widths. Those are proposed checks; this record contains historical source claims only. The complete commit touches an executable-mode file that this collection’s exact renderer does not support, so it is retained as reasoning instead of dropping that edit.'),
('a1d2a02dfb7c','diagnose','correctness',
 'Gate and up inputs near 400 are finite in FP16, and the eventual scaled SwiGLU result may also fit FP16, yet the fused path overflows. Explain the failure and why matching the old low-precision chain is the wrong oracle.',
 'The unscaled product can overflow before the later scale shrinks it. Checking only input or final-output ranges misses this intermediate. Keep the product in a wider register until applying the scale, then round at the specified output boundary. This intentionally differs from a chain that already overflowed; byte equality to that chain would preserve the defect. Test finite large products, scale values that rescue or still overflow the result, sign and activation behavior, and use a higher-precision formula as the correctness reference. Source-reported KLD and throughput results are evidence for that captured workload, not proof for every model. No local GPU result is claimed.'),
('1cc5c3079c14','select_dispatch','tradeoff',
 'A relaxed matrix-unit softmax product passes RMS error but makes 10–25% of elements a quarter-ulp worse; another head-width variant is 3.2x slower. What should be retained from those failures?',
 'Aggregate RMS can conceal elementwise deterioration. The accepted source route represents probability as high and low BF16 terms and checks both maximum and RMS error against FP64 truth, while retaining FP32 online-softmax carries. That does not imply universal bitwise equality: the reported teacher KLD is 0.0780 versus 0.0770 for the SIMD arm. The head-width 256 variant stays on stock attention after its 3.2x slowdown; a win at 192/128 is not a universal geometry result. A future validation should cover large logits, ragged tiles, the exact runtime probe and complete forward quality. These are prospective checks, while all timings and KLD values in this record are historical author reports.'),
('9c511c4710d9','review_correctness','correctness',
 'A proposed GDN precision fix changes state and decay to FP32, but a follow-up restores the Hadamard packs’ gate dtype. Explain why “always use the state dtype” was not a sufficient rule.',
 'For the cited qwen4_exp packs, BF16 spacing below one rounds some decay gates to exactly one, erasing intended forgetting; the reference declares FP32 state. Every gate producer and persisted checkpoint namespace must agree with the new contract. But another pack can have FP32 state while intentionally using a different gate precision. The captured follow-up therefore keeps the Hadamard pack’s established gate path instead of blindly tying it to state dtype. It also permits four FP32 eps in the compiled-versus-chain transcendental probe after a reported three-ulp toolchain difference. Test both pack classes and all decode/verify/prefill producers, and distinguish mathematical intent from bitwise compiler agreement. The author report is not a new local quality measurement.'),
('41896655a01a','diagnose','correctness',
 'Joined projections are byte-equal to solo projections, yet grouped verification differs after row zero. Explain the two route-dependent causes and why the source did not simply cap all joins at eight rows.',
 'Equal projected inputs do not guarantee the same downstream recurrence or reduction. One guard excluded the fused GDN verify kernel whenever projected slices were supplied, forcing grouped calls onto a differently rounded chain; removing that unnecessary guard aligns those routes. A separate MoE boundary switches from the rows kernel to sorted verification past eight rows. Capping every join at eight made larger grouped work slower, so the source kept that switch and tested each arm against its own declared reference rather than pretending the two reductions are universally identical. The remaining eight model-gated test failures also existed on main; the report is not an all-tests-clean claim. A disconfirming regression should cover both sides of each routing condition.'),
('ff7f359bd024','review_correctness','correctness',
 'An optional-kernel canary resets Metal’s error latch after its probe. Why can that make a fallback less safe, and what independent shape/stride bugs were addressed alongside it?',
 'A canary may encounter a latch raised by an earlier operation. Clearing it unconditionally hides the original forward failure. Record the pending state before the probe and clear only a newly raised probe error, retaining unrelated failures. Separately, caching output-bearing configurations by a growing row count can leak plans across chunk lengths, and a strided row-index view cannot be interpreted as contiguous without an explicit request or layout gate. Test preexisting versus new latch states, repeated chunk lengths and strided indices independently. This is an error-ownership and layout repair; dispatch fallback alone is not proof of safety, and no timing gain is claimed here.'),
('fa76a4b50b3f','diagnose','correctness',
 'How can a three-row recurrent-state tail retain gigabytes of prefill memory, and why must a copy-based repair change evaluation cadence and admission accounting together?',
 'A small view can keep its entire backing prefill array alive in every layer. The source makes the tail an owned copy and includes it in the layer-loop evaluation cadence so the intended lifetime reduction actually happens. Admission should then charge the remaining cadence window rather than the old per-layer pin; otherwise the runtime saves memory but still rejects feasible work. The author reports byte-identical output, unchanged prefill speed and about 2.5 GB lower peak at width 4096. The same commit also changes KV/admission policy, so those distinct changes should not be credited to the tail copy without an ablation. Validate ownership/lifetime and every cache format, and measure real peak separately from the planner’s estimate.'),
('5b22ed84dfb5','diagnose','positive',
 'A BF16 LoRA bypass allocates a 1.3–1.8 GB temporary after multiplication by a scalar. What explains the large observed speed gain, and how should the smaller kernel-fusion contribution be separated?',
 'The source identifies an FP32 scalar array promoting the full low-rank result, not expensive LoRA arithmetic alone. Accumulate with addmm into the base output’s dtype, preserving the intended precision and avoiding the huge promoted tensor. The reported main improvement is roughly 18.5 to 10.2 seconds per step; the subsequent DiT glue fusions improve 10.2 to 9.9. Thus most of the bundle gain belongs to avoiding promotion, not the three new kernels. Test actual tensor dtype/shape and the 15406-row workload, retain the unfused chain as oracle, and measure the two mechanisms separately. These figures and byte-identical-video claims are source reports, not a local reproduction.'),
('d569d05aec23','interpret_benchmark','tradeoff',
 'Batching speculative recurrence and attention removes thousands of short dispatches. A cycle still takes 158 ms for 1.50 tokens per stream versus 92 ms for a plain step. Should this enable speculation by default?',
 'No. Amortized cost is about 105.3 ms per accepted token, still worse than the 92 ms plain step in that reported regime. The no-draft batched trunk at 89 ms shows dispatch consolidation worked, but draft rows still pay expensive MoE passes; acceptance is the limiting factor. Keep this as a measured bottleneck/tradeoff rather than a positive universal speed target. A valid policy needs workload-specific acceptance and whole-cycle timing, plus exact snapshot/state semantics and lossless greedy validation. The arithmetic comparison follows the quoted source numbers; no model execution or new speed measurement is implied.'),
('3e068bb0e771','interpret_benchmark','neutral',
 'Attention dispatches are batched across sixteen sessions, but the complete step remains 1.00x. What is the useful lesson and which correctness properties still matter?',
 'The measured attention portion was only about 6.5 ms of a 110 ms step, and the old dispatch grids were not the assumed latency bottleneck. Fewer dispatches therefore did not establish a whole-step win. Preserve this neutral result instead of relabeling landing the change as performance success. Each row still needs its own cache address, split count and causal position; dense, block-completing and sparse cases must agree with their single-row references. Further profiling should target the dominant component or a different demonstrated latency-bound regime. Those are recommendations; the 1.00x result and byte-parity claims remain historical author evidence.'),
('f26aaa4ed836','review_correctness','correctness',
 'A table-producer route may discover missing scratch only after encoding has begun, while a rotated plan writes tables it never uses. Separate the correctness contract from the neutral timing result.',
 'Admission must establish required scratch before any work is encoded so failure cannot leave a partially scheduled operation. A rotated plain plan should not produce unused tables merely because another plan needs them. The source reports avoiding about 60 MB of unnecessary writes but a neutral timing result; saved traffic is a mechanism, not a measured speedup. Test zero/missing scratch, rotated and table-consuming plans, and ensure refusal happens before encoding without hiding unrelated errors. Keep the unchanged target arithmetic and source validation limits explicit.'),
('ec9ff36cf41e','interpret_benchmark','neutral',
 'A unified generic Q4 loop is 1–3% slower without inlining and neutral within A/A spread after inlining. What result should a curator and optimization agent record?',
 'Record a neutral refactor in the inlined regime and a regression in the tested uninlined regime. Do not turn fewer code paths or a cleaner abstraction into an invented performance win. The compiler’s inlining/resource behavior is part of the result, so keep the tested compiler, geometry and numerical contract attached. A proposed follow-up would compare assembly/resource use and controlled paired timings with a predeclared minimum useful improvement; those checks were not performed by this library. The losing and null observations remain useful evidence for rejecting a simplistic “generic loop is faster” hypothesis.'),
('c2aa3aaf2715','review_correctness','correctness',
 'One of two GDN weight-preparation decay writers rejects nonfinite values while the other does not. Why is fixing only the failing call site an incomplete repair?',
 'Duplicated producers can encode inconsistent invariants and drift again. Route them through the same checked writer so nonfinite input and overflow rejection apply to both, while preserving the declared BF16-widening and E2M1 table interpretation. Test both producer entry points with finite boundaries and explicit nonfinite/overflow cases; do not treat a GPU kernel parity test as coverage of host-side weight preparation. This is a correctness/data-preparation change. The source supplies no new runtime throughput win, and none is invented here.')]
